cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use LazyQC in your research, please cite the paper below."
title: "LazyQC"
abstract: "Variational quantum algorithms generate batches of expectation-value requests across observables, data examples and parameter shifts. Existing causal-cone reductions optimize individual requests but may repeat analysis and simulation across a batch. LazyQC schedules these requests using structure-keyed cone analysis, grouping of identical bound reduced circuits, an analysis-aware dense fallback and width-based CPU/GPU placement. For a 3,220-request Jacobian workload, LazyQC reduces analysis time from 6.22 s to 22.8 ms and total time from 6.98 s to 128 ms relative to per-request Qiskit LightCone reduction with deduplication and Aer GPU execution. At 28 qubits it outperforms direct GPU simulation by factors of 51–95 on an H100 for two depth-four local circuit families. At 128 logical qubits, narrow-cone batches complete in 12–35 ms versus 206–274 ms for a shared untruncated matrix-product state. Overall, LazyQC can achieve substantial speedups over direct simulation when the computational savings outweigh the analysis overhead."
